<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Environment Steps in Offline Agents</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tracking performance metrics relative to environment steps during online training is standard practice in the RL community. This methodology is also applied in the analysis of the offline Tandem agent in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ostrovski et al. (2021)</a></cite>, which closely mirrors the behavior of its Active counterpart.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the Passive agent—by definition—does not interact with the environment and thus cannot influence environment steps. This poses a challenge for directly comparing its performance with that of the Active and Tandem agents. To ensure comparability across training procedures, we allow the Passive agent to interact with the environment during training in the same manner as an online agent, but without adding the resulting interaction data into its replay buffer. This setup enables the Passive agent to remain trained solely on an offline dataset while allowing performance comparisons based on environment steps, with only minimal code changes required.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Pseudocode of methods</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We add the pseudocode of the Active, Passive, and Tandem agents (in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ A.5 Pseudocode of methods ‣ Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ Offline vs. Online Learning in Model-based RL: Lessons for Data Collection Strategies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the variation of Passive and Tandem agents (in Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ A.5 Pseudocode of methods ‣ Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ Offline vs. Online Learning in Model-based RL: Lessons for Data Collection Strategies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) used in decoupled analysis (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS5.SSS1" title="3.5.1 OODin MBRL ‣ 3.5 Deep Dive into Performance Degradation ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Offline vs. Online Learning in Model-based RL: Lessons for Data Collection Strategies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5.1</span></a>), as well as the second remedy (in Alg. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ A.5 Pseudocode of methods ‣ Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ Offline vs. Online Learning in Model-based RL: Lessons for Data Collection Strategies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) for better clarity.
Below, the training of the world model <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> includes training all components in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ DreamerV3 ‣ 2.1 Preliminaries ‣ 2 Method ‣ Offline vs. Online Learning in Model-based RL: Lessons for Data Collection Strategies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), while training <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> includes all components in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ DreamerV3 ‣ 2.1 Preliminaries ‣ 2 Method ‣ Offline vs. Online Learning in Model-based RL: Lessons for Data Collection Strategies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Learning agents</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_figure_panel ltx_role_start_3_columns"></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="alg1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#E06363;">Active Agent</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div>
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize:</span> Replay buffer <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> = a few random episodes.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            World model <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> + Policy <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> by seed <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E06363;" mathcolor="#E06363">S</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E06363;" mathcolor="#E06363">A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each step <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             Sample <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.8789,0.3867,0.3867}\mathcal{D}^{i}_{A}}\sim\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E06363;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#E06363">𝒟</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E06363;" mathcolor="#E06363">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E06363;" mathcolor="#E06363">i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.8789,0.3867,0.3867}\mathcal{D}^{i}_{A}}\sim\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>

